{"id":"f6ccf243-4eea-4de7-bd8e-205a928d6471","object":"chat.completion","created":1784832357,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = n\\n        par = n.get('parent')\\n        if par is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(par, []).append(nid)\\n    # precompute all descendants for each node (including itself)\\n    descendants = {}\\n    def get_desc(nid):\\n        if nid in descendants:\\n            return descendants[nid]\\n        res = {nid}\\n        for c in children.get(nid, []):\\n            res |= get_desc(c)\\n        descendants[nid] = res\\n        return res\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        get_desc(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n    retained_symbols = set(n['symbol'] for n in nodes)\\n    removal_order = []\\n    # order by depth descending, then id ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required:\\n            continue\\n        # check children still retained\\n        has_child = any(c in retained for c in children.get(nid, []))\\n        if has_child:\\n            continue\\n        # nodes to remove (descendants still present)\\n        to_remove = [d for d in descendants[nid] if d in retained]\\n        remove_symbols = set(node_map[d]['symbol'] for d in to_remove)\\n        new_symbols = retained_symbols - remove_symbols\\n        if required.issubset(new_symbols):\\n            for d in to_remove:\\n                retained.discard(d)\\n            retained_symbols = new_symbols\\n            removal_order.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"retained_ids\\\": retained_ids, \\\"removal_order\\\": removal_order}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that performs grammar-guided node reduction on a tree. The task is SE-PE-01, described as \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\". The input case has \"nodes\" with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also \"required_symbols\" list. We need to produce a result that includes sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order. The problem says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So the output should be a dictionary with keys \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\". Possibly also something else? The task description says: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we need to compute which nodes to keep and in what order they were removed. The algorithm: perform grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving interestingness oracle. The interestingness oracle is that all required symbols must remain represented. Also grammar_valid: the tree after removal must still be a valid tree? Possibly the grammar is implicit: each node has a symbol, and the parent-child relationships define a tree. The operation: tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented. Also consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes. Use node ID as stable tie break at equal depth. So we need to iterate over nodes in order of depth descending, and for nodes with same depth, by ID? Since depth is given, we can group by depth. For each node (excluding root? root is not removable), check if it is removable (removable flag true) and if removing it would still leave all required symbols represented across the tree (i.e., after removal, the set of symbols of all remaining nodes must include all required_symbols). Also need to consider that when a node is removed, its children are also removed? In tree reduction, removing a node typically removes its entire subtree. So if we remove a node, all its descendants are also removed. But careful: the task says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Wait: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That suggests that we should not remove a node if it has children that are still present (retained). But in typical reduction, if we remove a node, we remove its whole subtree, so children are not retained. But the instruction says \"or a node with retained children\" meaning we cannot remove a node if any of its children are still in the tree? That would prevent removal of internal nodes unless all children are also removed first. That makes sense: we should process bottom-up: only consider removing a node if all its children have already been removed (or are also flagged for removal?). The algorithm: we need to decide the removal order. Since we consider deeper nodes first, we will try to remove leaf nodes first. If a leaf node is removable, we remove it. Then we can consider its parent later. So we need to simulate the process: start with all nodes present. Iterate over nodes in descending depth order (and tie break by ID). For each node that is still present, and is removable, and is a leaf? Actually \"a node with retained children\" means if any child is still present, we cannot remove it. So we only consider nodes that have no children present (i.e., leaf nodes) or whose children have all been removed. So we need to check current set of retained nodes. So we do a greedy removal: for each node in that order, if it is removable and its removal (and its subtree) does not cause required symbols to be missing, and it has no children currently retained, then remove it (i.e., mark as removed, add to removal_order). Continue until no more nodes can be removed. Then the retained set is all nodes not removed. Then output sorted retained IDs (by ID? \"sorted retained IDs