ase): Input case has \"nodes\" list, each with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. \"required_symbols\" list. We'll parse them into a dict of node objects. We'll also build adjacency: parent -> children. Initially all nodes are retained. We'll have a set of retained_ids. We'll also maintain for each node its descendants (subtree) to quickly compute symbols removed? Or we can recompute set of symbols each time? The number of nodes is small (7 in fixtures), so we can just recompute. But we need to be careful: when we remove a node, we also remove its descendants. So we need to know which descendants are still present. Since we process bottom-up, if a child is already removed, it's not in retained set. So removal of a parent will remove the parent and any remaining descendants (which should be none if we removed children first). But to be safe, we can compute the set of nodes to be removed when removing a given node: that node plus all its descendants that are still retained. Then check if any of those nodes are required symbols? Actually the required symbols check is about the entire tree after removal: we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol still retained. So we can check by comparing the set of symbols of retained nodes before and after removal. If any required symbol is only present in the set of nodes to be removed, then removal is invalid.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n1. Build mapping: node_id -> node dict.\n2. Build children mapping: parent -> list of children IDs.\n3. Compute subtree set for each node (including itself). Since tree is small, we can compute recursively or using depth order. But we also need to update as nodes are removed. Simpler: each time we consider a node, compute the set of nodes that would be removed: node_id plus all its descendants that are still in retained set. We can compute descendants by traversing the full tree from that node, but only if the descendant is still retained. Since we process bottom-up, most descendants will already be removed. We can precompute all descendants (full subtree) and then filter by retained set. Precomputing full subtree (all descendants) is okay because they don't change (the tree structure is static). So we compute for each node a set of descendant IDs (including itself). Then during removal check, we take the intersection of that set with retained set to get the actual nodes that will be removed. Then check symbols.\n\n4. Determine order: sort all nodes by depth descending, then by id ascending. (Root depth 0, others depth 1,2). But note that root is not removable (removable false). So we skip root.\n\n5. Iterate over that list. For each node, if it is still retained and removable and has no retained children (i.e., for all children of this node, they are not in retained set), then compute the set of nodes to be removed (node + retained descendants). Check if after removal, the required symbols are still all present. If yes, then remove: for each id in removal set, remove from retained set, and record the node id (the one we removed) in removal_order list. But careful: removal set includes the node and its descendants. The \"actual removal order\" should list only the node ID that we tentatively removed? Or the IDs of all removed nodes? The phrase \"the actual removal order\" likely means the order in which nodes were removed (the ones we decided to remove). Since we remove a node and its subtree, the subtree nodes are removed as a consequence. Do we list them? Typically in reduction, we record the order of removals of the nodes we explicitly removed (the ones we chose). The children are removed implicitly. The task says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" I think the removal order is the list of node IDs that we actively chose to remove, in the order they were removed. The children are not individually recorded because they are removed as part of that operation. So we only add the no