at symbol. That seems to be a hard constraint. However, the subsequent line says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That might be a softening? Or maybe it's a condition to check after the prohibition? I think the prohibition is absolute. To be safe, we should interpret \"a required symbol\" as a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. So we should not remove such nodes. Let's look at the fixtures: In first case, required_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]. root is already not removable. s1 appears in nodes n1 (depth1, removable false) and n4 (depth2, removable true). So n4 has symbol \"s1\" which is required. Should n4 be removable? According to the prohibition, we cannot remove n4 because its symbol is required. In the results? Not given. But we can infer from typical reduction: you would not remove a node that is the only provider of a required symbol, but if there are multiple, you could remove one and keep the other. The prohibition might be misinterpreted. The phrase \"a required symbol\" could mean the node itself is required (like the root is required, and a required symbol node is also required). But the root is special because its symbol is \"root\" which is in required_symbols. So maybe \"required symbol\" refers to nodes that have a symbol listed in required_symbols. So they are considered essential and cannot be removed. That would mean n4 should not be removed because it has symbol \"s1\". However, n1 also has symbol \"s1\" and is not removable. So there are two nodes with s1. But the prohibition says never remove a required symbol, regardless of duplicates. So n4 cannot be removed. But then why is n4 marked removable? The fixture has \"removable\": true for n4. That suggests that the field indicates whether the node is allowed to be removed by the algorithm? Or it's a property of the node? The problem says \"removable\" field is given. So if removable is false, we cannot remove. If true, we may consider. So n4 is removable true. So maybe the prohibition \"Never remove a required symbol\" is already encoded in the removable flag? But n4 is removable true, so it's allowed to be removed? That contradicts. Let's re-read the instruction: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" This is a directive to our algorithm. It might be independent of the removable flag. The removable flag might indicate that the node is a candidate for removal, but we still must respect these rules. So n4 has symbol s1 which is required, so we must not remove it even though removable is true. That means the algorithm should skip n4. In the fixture, n4 is removable true, but we should not remove it because its symbol is required. So the algorithm will not remove n4. That seems plausible. Let's check n2 in first case: symbol s2, not required, removable true. That's a candidate. So we'll implement the prohibition: if node.symbol in required_symbols or node.id == \"root\" (root is not removable anyway), then we skip.\n\nBut note: root's symbol is \"root\" which is in required_symbols. So skip root anyway.\n\nNow about \"a node with retained children\": we already handle that.\n\nSo algorithm becomes:\n\n- Precompute: required_symbols set.\n- For each node in sorted order:\n   if node is still retained and node.removable and node.symbol not in required_symbols:\n        # check retained children\n        children = children_map[node.id]\n        has_retained_child = any(child_id in retained_set for child_id in children)\n        if not has_retained_child:\n            # compute nodes to remove: node + all descendants in retained_set\n            to_remove = get_descendants_set(node.id) & retained_set\n            # check if removing these nodes would cause any required symbol to be missing\n            # Get symbols of to_remove nodes, and also symbols of all retained nodes (current).\n            # We can compute symbols after removal: current_symbols - symbols_of_to_rem