ever remove\n    children = children_map.get(node_id, [])\n    has_child = any(cid in retained_set for cid in children)\n    if has_child: continue\n    # compute to_remove = desc_set[node_id] & retained_set\n    to_remove = [nid for nid in desc_set[node_id] if nid in retained_set]  # or set intersection\n    # compute symbols of to_remove\n    remove_symbols = set(node_dict[nid]['symbol'] for nid in to_remove)\n    # after removal symbols\n    new_symbols = retained_symbols - remove_symbols\n    if required_symbols.issubset(new_symbols):\n        # do removal\n        for nid in to_remove:\n            retained_set.discard(nid)\n            # remove symbol from retained_symbols? but we already computed new_symbols, so we can just set retained_symbols = new_symbols after removal\n        retained_symbols = new_symbols\n        removal_order.append(node_id)\n\n- After loop: retained_ids = sorted(list(retained_set)) (string sort)\n- return {\"retained_ids\": retained_ids, \"removal_order\": removal_order}\n\nCheck: When we remove a node, its children might have been already removed earlier (since we process bottom-up, we would have removed them first). So to_remove will only be the node itself (since children already removed). But our code handles the general case.\n\nAlso we must ensure we never remove the root: root's removable is false, and its symbol is in required_symbols, so it's skipped.\n\nNow we need to wrap in solve(case) function that returns a JSON-serializable object. Exactly as described.\n\nThe output should be a JSON object with keys \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\". The examples from the task description? Not provided but we can infer.\n\nNow produce the one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value a Python source string. Escape properly.\n\nWe must not include any extra text. The response should be a valid JSON object with single key \"implementation\". The Python code should be valid and have no syntax errors.\n\nLet's write the code.